    "evidence_text": "Parallel processing in the mammalian retina\n\nGuidance of B cells by the orphan G protein-coupled receptor EBI2 shapes humoral immune responses.\n\nHumoral immunity depends on both rapid and long-term antibody production against invading pathogens.\n\nPatch-clamp recordings were made from retinal ganglion cells in the mouse retina.\n\nEmbryonic Origin of Postnatal Neural Stem Cells\n\nThe location of B1 cells determines the types of OB neurons they generate.\n\nSpecific deficit of the ON response in visual transmission by targeted disruption of the mGIuR6 gene\n\nThe homozygous mutant mice showed a loss of ON responses but unchanged OFF responses to light.\n\nThe results demonstrate that mGluR6 is essential in synaptic transmission to the ON bipolar cell and that the OFF response provides an important means for transmitting visual information.\n\nVision tests in the mouse: Functional phenotyping with electroretinography.\n\nA naturally occurring mouse model of X-linked congenital stationary night blindness.\n\nTo evaluate visual transmission, cortical potentials were recorded with a scalp electrode.\n\nRetinal histologic analysis was performed by light microscopy.   \n\n\nVisually-driven cortical activity was also abnormal in nob animals.\n\nThis signaling system is required for normal vascular development in the retina and for vascular survival in the cochlea.",
